 - `Task.replay(lines)` is `Item.replay` (inherited): `Item.Store.read` runs the same `replay_line` per entry in the browser. Task only overrides `replay_new`, `replay_line` and `replay_end` for the legacy mapping. It builds a task from parsed `task.jsonl` objects, using each line's own time (`at`, `assign.at|landed_at|requested_at`), never the wall clock, so the same file always gives the same task. Explicit lines (`{"start":{}}`, `pause`, `resume`, `stop`, `complete` (old `finish` too), `wait`, `approve`, `improve`, `reject`) call the method of that name; once one appears, they decide the state.
 - No explicit line (an old file): `landed_at` alone is `complete`, `closed_by` is `stopped`, anything else is `running`. `assign` fields merge into `data` (latest wins; `session_id` becomes `session`).
 - `waiting` is a sixth state: `wait({on, what})` (from idle, running, paused), then a verdict, each stored as `verdict: {is, note, by}`: `approve(note?)` (to running; a queue that delays it to idle does not exist yet), `improve(note)` (to running, to revise and wait again), `reject(note)` (to stopped). `stop` and `complete` accept it.
-- **Homes and references (step 14a):** `tasks.add` through the item tool makes the child its own folder, `<module>/tasks/<slug>/` when `public/framework/<module>/` exists (never created), else `<parent folder>/<slug>/`. Line 1 is `{id, path, parent, title, module, …}`; the parent gets one `{"task": "<app path>"}` line and no copied data. Replay turns each such line into a live child with `Task.from_ref(path)`, using `Task.resolver` (node sets it, `page_tools.link_refs`); a missing file gives a stub child with `state: "missing"`. Old inline `{"tasks":{"add":…}}` lines still replay inline. A reference may carry `as`, the id the child had before it moved (`{"task": url, "as": "x"}`, written by the item tool's `move` key); replay then uses that id instead of the folder name, so a moved child keeps its id ([core/Item, moving](../Item/doc/architecture.md#an-item-moves-home-with-its-id-its-history-and-one-reference)). A reference with `"type":"Session","spawn":{…}` is a Session entry: `from_ref(ref, as, entry)` builds that class and keeps `spawn` on the child, and a child whose record holds a spawn's `{assign}` line is a Session too ([core/Session, "Session entries"](../Session/readme.md#session-entries)). The browser Store resolving them is step 14b.
+- **Homes and references (step 14a; changed 2026-10-26):** a task stays in the session that files it. `tasks.add` through the item tool nests the child in the parent's `task.jsonl`; only an explicit `home: "<module>"` makes its own folder `<module>/tasks/<slug>/` (when `public/framework/<module>/` exists, never created), and the call's path never picks a module. `module` is a tag the module page finds the task by. A child folder, when made, has line 1 `{id, path, parent, title, module, …}`; the parent gets one `{"task": "<app path>"}` line and no copied data. Replay turns each such line into a live child with `Task.from_ref(path)`, using `Task.resolver` (node sets it, `page_tools.link_refs`); a missing file gives a stub child with `state: "missing"`. Old inline `{"tasks":{"add":…}}` lines still replay inline. A reference may carry `as`, the id the child had before it moved (`{"task": url, "as": "x"}`, written by the item tool's `move` key); replay then uses that id instead of the folder name, so a moved child keeps its id ([core/Item, moving](../Item/doc/architecture.md#an-item-moves-home-with-its-id-its-history-and-one-reference)). A reference with `"type":"Session","spawn":{…}` is a Session entry: `from_ref(ref, as, entry)` builds that class and keeps `spawn` on the child, and a child whose record holds a spawn's `{assign}` line is a Session too ([core/Session, "Session entries"](../Session/readme.md#session-entries)). The browser Store resolving them is step 14b.
 - `tasks` is made on first use, so a task with no subtasks costs no `List`.
 - **Migration:** [`Server/TaskMigrate.mjs`](/Server/TaskMigrate.mjs) gives an old session task whose module is clear a home here. The home's `sessions` field (`[{id, task}]`) names the old folder, and the old folder gains one `{"home":"<url>"}` line, kept as `data.home`. It runs as a dry run by default and never deletes. [Plan](/framework/servex/fs/?file=public/framework/ai/sessions/2026/10/16/voice-20261016/task-migrate/plan.md) · [dry run](/framework/servex/fs/?file=public/framework/ai/sessions/2026/10/16/voice-20261016/task-migrate/dry-run.md).
 
